stage('Tests') {
  parallel {
    stage('Unit')        { steps { sh 'mvn test' } }
    stage('Integration') { steps { sh 'mvn verify -Pit' } }
  }
}

stage('Compatibility') {
  matrix {
    axes {
      axis { name 'JDK';  values '17', '21' }
      axis { name 'DB';   values 'mysql', 'postgres' }
    }
    excludes {
      exclude {
        axis { name 'JDK'; values '17' }
        axis { name 'DB';  values 'postgres' }
      }
    }
    agent { docker { image "maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-${JDK}" } }
    stages {
      stage('Test') { steps { sh 'mvn -B verify -Pdb-$DB' } }
    }
  }
}
matrix creates one parallel branch per combination, here three. Each axis value is available as an environment variable.

| when { branch 'main' } | script { if (...) { } }
Syntax | Declarative | Groovy inside a script step
When the condition is false | Stage is shown as skipped | Stage runs and shows green, doing nothing
Agent allocation | Can be avoided with beforeAgent true | Agent is already allocated
Built-in conditions | branch, changeRequest, environment, expression, tag, changeset, allOf, anyOf, not | Whatever you code
Readability | Clear at a glance in the stage view | Hidden logic
stage('Deploy') {
  when { beforeAgent true; branch 'main' }
  agent { label 'deploy' }
  steps { sh './deploy.sh' }
}
Rule of thumb: decide whether a stage runs with when; use if only for logic inside a stage that must run anyway.

| try/catch (script) | catchError | post { failure }
Purpose | Handle an error in code | Let the pipeline continue but record the failure | React after the stage or pipeline ends
Build result after error | Unchanged (SUCCESS) unless you set it | Set by buildResult / stageResult | Already decided; post only reacts
Next steps run | Yes | Yes | Only post steps
Typical use | Fallback logic | Optional scan that should not stop the build | Notify Slack, publish reports, clean up
catchError(buildResult: 'UNSTABLE', stageResult: 'FAILURE') {
  sh 'mvn dependency-check:check'
}
Rule of thumb: prefer catchError and post in Declarative pipelines; a bare try/catch often hides real failures, turns red builds green, and can even swallow a user clicking Abort.
